---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const steps = [
  { n: "01", t: "Shade",    s: ["Covered for the last", "20 to 40 days"] },
  { n: "02", t: "Pick",     s: ["Youngest leaves,", "first harvest only"] },
  { n: "03", t: "Steam",    s: ["Within hours,", "to stop oxidation"] },
  { n: "04", t: "Dry",      s: ["Cooled, then", "dried flat"] },
  { n: "05", t: "Destem",   s: ["Stem and vein out.", "This is tencha"] },
  { n: "06", t: "Grind",    s: ["Granite, slowly.", "30 to 40g an hour"] },
];

const essays = [
  {
    title: "My Lifelong Career as a Reader and Writer",
    where: "Translingual, Issue 6, page 26",
    href: "https://ielp.ehe.osu.edu/files/2026/03/Translingual-Issue-6.pdf",
    note: "How reading arrived secondhand, from a brother whose books I was given after him, and what it took to make it mine.",
  },
  {
    title: "A Film Analysis: Grave of the Fireflies",
    where: "Translingual, Issue 2, April 2023",
    note: "An antiwar film that haunts without a single scare, and why that stays with a person far longer than fright does.",
  },
  {
    title: "Shades of Black",
    where: "Translingual, Issue 2, April 2023",
    note: "Someone said I dressed like Dracula. The colour turned out to be doing more work than either of us assumed.",
  },
  {
    title: "Imprisonment in The Allegory of the Cave and The House of Asterion",
    where: "Unpublished",
    note: "Plato and Borges arriving at the same question from opposite ends: whether someone who cannot see the walls is still imprisoned by them.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="The Rough"
  description="Where the green and the stone on this site came from. Matcha as a morning ritual, and four essays. By Linh (Min) Le."
>
  <article class="rough">

    <header class="band g-ivory rough-head">
      <div class="shell">
        <p class="eyebrow">Beyond work</p>
        <h1>The Rough</h1>
        <p class="rough-lede">
          This site is green and its mark is a stone, and neither of those was settled at a desk.
          Both arrived from the parts of my life nobody is paying for, and it seemed dishonest to
          let the design imply otherwise.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="band plated g-leaf" style="--plate: var(--g-beige);">
      <div class="shell prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Matcha</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>Hardly any of what makes it good was decided by me</h2>

        <p>
          I make it every morning. There is a full set at home, the bowl and the whisk, and a corner
          of the kitchen that has been given over to nothing else, and the twenty minutes it asks of
          me are the only part of the day whose shape I can predict. On the mornings I skip it, I am
          never calmer for the time I saved.
        </p>
        <p>
          What holds me is not the drink waiting at the end of it. It is that almost everything
          determining whether that drink is any good had been settled months earlier, in a field I
          have never seen, by people whose names I will never know, through a sequence that offers no
          way back.
        </p>

        <figure class="fig">
          <div class="fig-frame">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 760 150" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" role="img"
                 aria-label="Six stages of matcha production, from shading in the field through to stone grinding.">
              {steps.map((st, i) => (
                <g class={st.t === "Grind" ? "st on" : "st"}>
                  <rect x={8 + i * 124} y="26" width="112" height="86" rx="7"/>
                  <text class="sn" x={20 + i * 124} y="44">{st.n}</text>
                  <text class="stt" x={20 + i * 124} y="63">{st.t}</text>
                  {st.s.map((ln, k) => (
                    <text class="sss" x={20 + i * 124} y={81 + k * 11}>{ln}</text>
                  ))}
                  {i < 5 && <path class="arw" d={`M${120 + i * 124} 69 l9 0 m-3.5 -3.5 l3.5 3.5 l-3.5 3.5`}/>}
                </g>
              ))}
              <text class="cap" x="8" y="136">Only after 05 is it tencha, which means tea for grinding. It is not matcha until 06.</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The plants are covered for the last twenty to forty days before picking, and starved of
          light they do something that reads almost like compensation: they push out chlorophyll and
          turn their catechins into L-theanine, which is why the powder comes out that green and why
          the bitterness gives way to something nearer sweetness. Only the youngest leaves of the
          first harvest are taken. They are steamed within hours so the leaf cannot begin oxidizing,
          then cooled, then dried, then stripped of stem and vein until nothing but leaf flesh
          remains.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then it passes between two granite stones turning slowly enough to yield thirty to forty
          grams in an hour. Speed is the thing they are deliberately giving up, because grinding
          faster makes heat, and heat carries the aroma off with it.
        </p>
        <p class="beat">By the time any of it reaches me, none of it is recoverable.</p>
        <p>
          What belongs to me is the last four minutes. The temperature of the water, the ratio, the
          way the whisk moves. Everything upstream has already fixed the ceiling, and no amount of
          attentiveness at my end will lift it.
        </p>
        <p>
          I have come to like work that behaves this way, where quality is settled early and quietly
          and the part anyone sees is small. If I am honest, it is also some of why a number that
          arrives already looking finished makes me uneasy rather than relieved.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is the only caffeine my body has ever agreed with, too. Coffee lends me an hour and then
          collects on the debt. Matcha carries its L-theanine alongside the caffeine, so the lift
          comes flatter and stays, which suits a morning that begins with reading something slowly.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band plated g-ivory" style="--plate: var(--g-leaf);">
      <div class="shell prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Stones</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>I collect the ones nobody prices highly</h2>

        <p>
          Not long after moving to Cincinnati I found GeoRarities, a rock shop tucked into a suite in
          an art studio building, the sort of place that does not announce itself and is better for
          it. I still go. Almost everything I own came out of small shops like that one rather than
          anywhere that would describe itself as a gallery.
        </p>
        <p>
          What I look for is semi-precious, which is a graceless phrase for stones that have been
          ranked and found to be worth less, and that ranking is most of why I like them. A stone
          whose price has already been settled invites you to admire the settlement. A semi-precious
          one leaves you alone with it, and what is left to go on is where it came from, how long the
          ground took to make it, and what people have believed about it for the last four thousand
          years. My element in Eastern philosophy is Water, so nearly everything I carry is blue or
          white.
        </p>
        <p>
          Lapis lazuli is the one I keep returning to. It is not a mineral at all but a rock, which
          is to say a committee: lazurite carrying the blue, calcite threaded so finely through it
          that you have to look for the white, and pyrite. The Egyptians set it into the burial masks
          of their royals and ground it into the most expensive blue any painter could buy. People
          confuse it with sodalite, which is a fair mistake in a dim room and an impossible one in
          sunlight, because sodalite carries almost no pyrite and lapis answers the light in small
          gold sparks that nothing else does.
        </p>
        <p class="beat">Its blue is the colour of a night that still has something in it.</p>
        <p>
          It is called the stone of wisdom, and the half of that claim I hold onto is the narrower
          one. The usual version has it sharpening rational thought, which is the part I already
          trust myself with. What I keep it for is what comes afterwards, the point at which a
          finding has to stop belonging to whoever ran the numbers and become something a room will
          act on. Whether or not a person believes a stone has any bearing on that, it is the harder
          half, and it is the half I keep choosing.
        </p>
        <p>
          The mark on this site is a stone being cut, and rough gathering into something finished and
          breaking apart again. That was not a metaphor I went looking for.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band plated g-cream" style="--plate: var(--g-ivory);">
      <div class="shell prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Writing</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>Before any of the analysis, there was this</h2>
        <p>
          Three of the four below appeared in Translingual, the journal of Ohio State&rsquo;s
          International English Language Programs. The fourth I never submitted anywhere, and it sits
          here because I have not managed to stop liking it.
        </p>

        <ol class="essays">
          {essays.map((e) => (
            <li class="essay">
              <h3 class="essay-title">
                {e.href ? <a href={e.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{e.title}</a> : e.title}
              </h3>
              <p class="essay-where figure">{e.where}</p>
              <p class="essay-note">{e.note}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band plated g-beige" style="--plate: var(--g-leaf);">
      <div class="shell prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">The throughline</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>I did not set out to have these agree with each other</h2>
        <p>
          Matcha taught me to be wary of a result that arrives already looking finished, because I
          know how much of it was settled long before anyone could see it. The stone I carry is kept
          for the part that comes after the thinking, when the worked-out thing has to be said with
          enough weight that somebody moves. And the writing came before any of the analysis, years
          of learning that a well-made sentence with nothing behind it is worse company than a plain
          one that means something.
        </p>
        <p>
          Which is more or less a description of what I do for a living. My degree is in Information
          Systems and my foundation is accounting, and the work I keep choosing sits at the place
          where a technical system has to turn into a decision a person can act on.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the Spotify case the part I cared most about was never the financial model, and I built
          the financial model. It was standing between behavioural science, product design and
          finance, three domains that turned up speaking different languages and disagreeing about
          what even counted as evidence, and carrying one argument across all of them until a
          stakeholder could follow it in a single sitting.
        </p>
        <p class="beat">
          I would rather be the person who makes a finding usable than the person who found it first.
        </p>
        <p>
          These three habits are where that came from, and I would keep all of them whether or not
          anybody was hiring.
        </p>
        <p class="closing-out">
          <a class="cta ghost" href="/work/compass-ai/">The case that argument was built for</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .rough-head { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem) clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem); }
  .rough-head h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-4);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -.02em;
    line-height: 1;
    margin-top: .3rem;
  }
  .rough-lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .prose { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -.015em;
    margin: .3rem 0 1.3rem;
    max-width: 22ch;
  }
  .prose p { margin-bottom: 1.05em; line-height: 1.62; }
  .prose .beat {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-variation-settings: "SOFT" 60, "WONK" 1;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .fig { margin: 1.6rem 0 1.8rem; }
  .fig-frame {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: .8rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .fig-frame svg { display: block; width: 100%; min-width: 34rem; height: auto; }
  .st rect { fill: none; stroke: var(--rule-strong); stroke-width: 1; }
  .st.on rect { fill: var(--accent-wash); stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .sn  { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 8px; fill: var(--ink-faint); letter-spacing: .1em; }
  .stt { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; fill: var(--ink); }
  .st.on .stt { fill: var(--accent-deep); }
  .sss { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 7.5px; fill: var(--ink-soft); }
  .cap { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 8px; fill: var(--ink-faint); letter-spacing: .04em; }
  .arw { fill: none; stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.2; stroke-linecap: round; stroke-linejoin: round; }

  .essays { list-style: none; margin: 1.4rem 0 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .essay { padding: 1.1rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .essay:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); }
  .essay-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-variation-settings: "SOFT" 40;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }
  .essay-title a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--accent-line); }
  .essay-title a:hover { color: var(--accent-deep); border-color: var(--accent); }
  .essay-where {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    letter-spacing: .05em;
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    margin-top: .3rem;
  }
  .essay-note { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--step--1); margin-top: .45rem; line-height: 1.55; }

  .closing-out { margin-top: .8rem; }
  .cta {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--2);
    letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; text-decoration: none;
    background: var(--accent-deep); color: var(--g-ivory);
    border: 1px solid var(--accent-deep); border-radius: 7px;
    padding: .6rem .95rem; display: inline-block;
    transition: background .25s ease, border-color .25s ease, color .25s ease;
  }
  .cta.ghost { background: transparent; color: var(--ink); border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 34%, transparent); }
  .cta.ghost:hover { background: var(--accent-wash); border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-deep); }
  .cta:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 3px; }
</style>
